# Class loading

Classes load **lazily** — on first use — via **class loaders**, turning a `.class` on disk into a live `Class` in the JVM.

## Three phases

- **Loading** — find + parse the bytes → a `Class` object
- **Linking** — **verify** bytecode, **prepare** statics, **resolve** references
- **Initialization** — run `static` blocks + static assignments

```java
class Config {
    static final int MAX = load();          // runs at INITIALIZATION
    static { System.out.println("init"); }  // ...on first use of Config
}
```

## The verifier

Before any bytecode runs, it's **proven safe** — no stack underflow, no bad jumps, no `int`-as-reference. Hostile `.class` rejected at load time.

## Loader hierarchy — parent-first delegation

**Bootstrap** (core JDK) → **Platform** (JDK modules) → **Application** (your classpath). A loader asks its **parent** first, so `java.lang.String` can't be counterfeited.

## Identity = name + loader

Same `.class`, two loaders → **two distinct types**. Powers **app servers** (per-app versions), **plugins**, **hot reload**.
